{
  "configuration_id": "config_006",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_001 (multimodal, singleprofile, scgpt, imagenet_vit_l, concat) achieved only 43.5% accuracy, which is low and suggests the single-profile architecture may be a bottleneck. With just one data point, the most important uncertainty is whether multiprofile aggregation—a major architectural axis—can substantially improve performance. Question tested: config_006 keeps the exact same RNA encoder (scgpt), image encoder (imagenet_vit_l), and fusion (concat) as config_001 but switches to multiprofile with avgpool aggregation, isolating the effect of multiprofile aggregation. Why this is the best next experiment: it is the minimal, most direct extension of the only completed experiment, so any accuracy change can be attributed to the multiprofile+aggregation shift. Avgpool is the cheapest aggregation (vs. transformer), keeping cost moderate while still testing the key hypothesis. If multiprofile helps, the remaining 7 experiments can exploit that direction (try transformer, weighted fusion, different encoders); if it does not, we learn the bottleneck lies in the encoders or fusion and can pivot. Expected relative cost: moderate increase over config_001 (~1.5–2×, roughly 1.4–1.9 GPU hours) due to processing multiple profiles and the avgpool aggregation step, but still well within budget with 8 experiments remaining."
}

